\startchapter[reference=sec:publist, title={Summary of publications}]

This chapter contains summaries of the articles
I made substantial contributions to during the course of my work as PhD candidate.

\startsection[title={\titlecite{angerer2016}}]

Summary:
In this article, we described the initial scope of \n{destiny}, an R package implementing diffusion maps.
Diffusion maps were chosen for their at the time unique ability to capture smooth nonlinear transitions in single cell transcription data and its robustness towards noise.
\n{Destiny} was the first user friendly and practical implementation of the algorithm, suited for both interactive use by data scientists and use in other packages.
Its practicality is a result of high performance making it scale from personal computers to high performance machines, as well as algorithmic enhancements adapting the algorithm to scRNA-Seq and qPCR data.

% efficiency
\n{Destiny}’s scalability and compuatational efficiency is mainly achieved by reusing the sparsity structure of the sparse distance matrix to perform element-wise operations.
To this end, \n{destiny} symmetrizes its kNN matrix and uses the symmetric variant of diffusion maps.
Other adaptations for efficiency include such initializing based on a k-nearest-neighbor search, full sparse matrix support for input data, as well as taking advantage of an approximate Eigen decomposition algorithmus.
This efficient design has been adopted by \n{scanpy} for its many tools that use a kNN search as preprocessing step.

% enhancements
\n{Destiny} also efficiently reuses intermediate results such as the transition matrix for its other functions.
Those include diffusion pseudo time (\n{DPT}, \inlinecite[haghverdi2016]) and the ability to project a second set of data into an existing diffusion map.
Apart from the sparsity support making it suitable for scRNA-Seq, explicit support for missing and censored values also make \n{destiny} fit to support qPCR data.
The R package is continuously maintained and has been adapted to make use of current best practices such as an adaptable diffusion map kernel, plotting with \n{ggplot2} and storing expression data in the \n{SingleCellExperiment} data structure.

% user friendly: defaults, no sigma
This integration and flexibility makes \n{destiny}n especially user friendly.
It is also easy to use without detail knowledge by being able to run without specifying parameters.
To this end, \n{destiny} locally scaled diffusion maps, and heuristically derives a fitting number of nearest neighbors.
I helped develop this nonparametric diffusion map variant, which is also used by \n{scanpy} today, during the parallel development of \n{destiny} and \n{DPT}.
When opting for a global scale instead, \n{destiny} will derive a suitable kernel width by default.

% plotting and color maps
\n{Destiny} contains dedicated plotting routines for diffusion maps and \n{DPT} that directly integrate into the popular plotting library \n{ggplot2}.
Destiny started providing perceptually continuous color-maps before they have been included in the major plotting libraries of Python and R.
Without them, accurate assessment of any kind of color-mapped visualizations cannot be guaranteed \cite[borkin2011,moreland2017].
\n{Destiny} supports \n{Bioconductor}’s annotated expression matrix classes as well as plain matrices and data frames.

% flexibility
\n{Destiny} can utilize a number of distance metrics including euclidean, cosine, and rank correlation distance.
Unlike other available R tools, \n{destiny} uses the chosen distance metric also for kNN search, which has been shown to improve results \cite[hu2016].
To make \n{destiny} deterministic, I contributed to a few of its dependencies to support random seeds.
\n{Destiny}’s API is flexible enough to be used as an analysis tool in its own right, as well as as a diffusion map backend for integrated analysis pipelines like \n{scater} \cite[mccarthy2017].
It also served as a flexible base for the implementation of \n{DPT} and gene relevance (see below).

Destiny has been cited 175 times at the time of writing and has for example been used for dimensional reduction and pseudo-time inference in analyses.
This includes hematopoietic stem and progenitor cell differentiation \cite[nestorowa2016], an immune atlas of clear cell renal cell carcinoma \cite[chevrier2017], development in the human adult brain \cite[lake2018] and an atlas of the aging drosophila brain \cite[davie2018].

My contribution:
First author

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{angerer2017}}]

Summary:
In this review article, we provide a selective view on the history of single cell data,
focusing on the growing number of captured cells per transcriptomics experiment.
We evaluate the reasons for capturing more cells, the qualitative changes in the captured data, and the consequences of these changes in terms of a shift in where challenges lie.
Capturing a high number of cells provided the necessary resulution to identify certain rare subpopulations, as well as for accurate inference of developmental trajectories.
As sequencing depth decreases just as cell numbers increase, data size per experiment does not change much.
However the tools to handle the data change, as e.g. expression matrices need to be stored in a sparse format which those tools need to support, and the noise per datum increases.
Challenges can be approached with a variety of attack angles, that usually range from emulation of older shapes, such as imputing noisy sparse data to make it smooth and dense, to approaches modelling the new properties.
The challenges can change rapidly: while smaller scRNA-Seq require regularization to handle their intrinsic “small n large p” properties, newer experiments capturing 10s of thousands of cells already no longer suffer from this problem.

My contribution:
First author

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{wolf2018}}]

Summary:
% reusable and remix-able workflow building blocks and data structures
In this article, we introduced \n{scanpy}, a framework for single cell analysis and visualization.
\n{Scanpy} is a framework for scRNA-Seq preprocessing, analysis, and visualization in Python.
\n{AnnData} is a data structure for annotated observation data including scRNA-Seq counts and expressions.
It is designed to be generic and independent of \n{scanpy}.
With F. Alexander Wolf I implemented the initial version of both, where he focused on performance while I focused on API and data structure design.
\n{Scanpy} is designed around \n{AnnData}, storing the results of each processing step in an expression matrix layer or an aligned annotation (see \infig[fig:anndata]).
\n{AnnData} can be stored on disk in various formats for snapshots, data interchange, and interoperability with command line tools.
For data too large to hold in memory, \n{AnnData} supports disk-backed operations.
For details about the implementation of \n{AnnData} and \n{scanpy}, see \in{Sections}[sec:anndata] and \in{}[sec:scanpy]

\n{AnnData}’s and \n{scanpy}’s modular and flexible design philosophies allows \n{scanpy} routines to be effortlessly interspersed with preprocessing and analysis steps of other tools, including R tools via \n{SingleCellExperiment} \cite[lun2017a,angerer2019b].
\n{AnnData} has been used for this purpose by several scRNA-Seq analysis tools since the publication of \n{scanpy}, such as \n{scVI} \cite[lopez2018], \n{DCA} \cite[eraslan2019], \n{DESC} \cite[li2019], or \n{BBKNN} \cite[polanski2020].
Its \type{.h5ad} format is supported by visual analysis pipelines like \n{Galaxy} \cite[afgan2018] or \n{cellxgene} \cite[megill2020].
\n{Scanpy} is widely used and popular in the scRNA-Seq analysis community \cite[zappia2018].
It was named Genome Biology’s milestone article of the year 2018 \cite[genomebiology2020].
It is integrated in \n{Galaxy}, used for large projects like the \n{Human Cell Atlas} \cite[regev2017] or \n{Tabula Muris} \cite[schaum2018], and at the time of writing cited by over 300 studies, review papers and tools.
So does EMBL-EBI’s expression atlas use \n{scanpy} for downstream analysis of all single cell experiments \cite[papatheodorou2020]

My contribution:
I designed the foundational APIs and \n{AnnData}.
I contributed to the implementation of some included algorithms.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{uzbas2019}}]

% flexible processing pipeline and Python library
Summary:
In this article, we developed BART-Seq, a targeted RNA-Seq protocol usable at the single cell or bulk levels.
See \inlinecite[uzbas2019] and \insec[sec:bart-seq] for BART-Seq, a transcriptomics protocol that allows to quantify many samples at once.
It is set apart from untargeted single cell and bulk RNA-Seq protocols due to its high sensitivity and low cost.
BART-Seq can use single cells or groups of multiple cells as samples.
Each of its samples is tagged by combining paired-end reads with pairs of computationally designed barcodes.
It is therefore possible distinguish high numbers of samples while sensitively measuring the specified target amplicons, such as expressed genes.
An advantage of the predictable barcoding over randomized barcodes is that sample identity is maintained, allowing for arbitrarily much information attached to each sample of a single batch, such as treatment labels.
I designed a demultiplexing and counting strategy for BART-Seq together with Fatma Uzbas and Micha Drucker (see \insec[sec:bart-seq]).
I implemented those strategies as well as visualization routines for the protocol as a Python package.
Part of this package is the BART-Seq equivalent to \n{Cell Ranger} or \n{zUMIs}:
a \n{Snakemake} pipeline \cite[koster2012] that performs all steps of the protocol to create count matrices, statistics and visualizations \cite[angerer2019a].

My contribution:
I helped design the demultiplexing and counting strategy.
I implemented a pipeline performing it and visualizing the result.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{angerer2020}}]

Summary:
% combines gene-level with cell-level analysis
In this article, we present gene relevance, a novel method for inference of the contributions individual variables have on the location of data in an embedding.
This algorithm is the latest addition to the \n{destiny} packge, building upon its efficient routines.
The method was inspired by PCA loadings, a intrinsic property of the linear dimensionality reduction method PCA.
These loadings are the linear factors scaling a variable to define its role in a principal component.
Using PCA as dimensional reduction method for expression data therefore allows to see each gene’s contribution to a cell’s position in a plot.
Gene relevance generalizes the idea to make it applicable to nonlinear dimension reduction methods.
It is designed to identify genes that drive the local positioning of a cell in an embedding by calculating the differential of each gene-cell combination.
This involves estimating the partial derivatives of the gene expression with respect to the embedding location by looking at cell neighborhoods.
This allows to detect locally relevant genes without a discrete clustering step, and therefore without data reuse as a result of using inferred cluster labels as input for gene level analysis.
The article shows robust derivation of both obvious candidates such as hemoglobin genes for hematopoietic development, as well as interesting but less obvious genes.
We also present an easy to use interface for calculating gene lists and plotting as part of the \n{destiny} package.

My contribution:
First author.
The initial idea comes from Fabian Theis, while I designed the changes making it applicable to real data with A. Scialdone and C. Marr and implemented it.
I also extended the idea to include scores for defined groups of cells (such as clusters) or the whole data for a global relevance score per gene.
The method is described in detail in \insec[sec:gene-relevance].
\n{Destiny} offers a rich and user friendly API for all gene relevance concepts, including plotting routines.

\stopsection

